course: dictionary
lesson: 16045
title: An old word for food
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words belong to old stories and old books. Victuals is one of them. It means food and drink, especially the food you take with you on a journey. «The sailors packed plenty of victuals before the long voyage.» Sailors need food for many weeks at sea, so victuals must mean food and drink for the trip.
explain: Victuals is nearly always used as a group, so it is usually plural. You will rarely hear someone say "a victual". The verb victual, meaning to supply food, is rare too. «The soldiers were victualled before they marched north.» They marched, so they needed food first. The context gives you the clue.
explain: Some old stories, such as The Wind in the Willows, use victuals to make speech sound grand and old-fashioned. In cowboy talk in the USA, people sometimes say «vittles» for the same word, as a joke. The spelling changes when it is said aloud, but the meaning stays the same.
fact: Victuals means food and drink, especially supplies for a journey or for many people.
fact: Victuals is usually used in the plural, as a group of food.
fact: Victuals is an old-fashioned word, found in old stories such as The Wind in the Willows.
fact: As a rare verb, victual means to supply a ship or an army with food.
fact: In cowboy talk in the USA, people sometimes say «vittles» for the same word, as a joke.
exercise: «The sailors loaded barrels of victuals onto the ship.» What does victuals mean? | Sails and ropes | Songs sung on deck | Heavy old boots | *Food and drink for a journey
exercise: «The soldiers were victualled before they marched.» What does victualled mean? | Taught to sing marching songs | Sent home to rest | *Given food and supplies | Tied up with rope
exercise: Cowboys in films sometimes say «vittles». What do they mean? | *Food and drink, said as a joke | A rope for catching cattle | A dusty old hat | A horse with a long tail
test: «Mum packed victuals for our train journey: sandwiches, apples and a flask of tea.» What are victuals in this sentence? | Tickets for the train | Maps of the countryside | *Food and drink for the journey | Blankets for sleeping
test: Why might an author use an old word like victuals in an old story? | Because it is the newest slang word | Because it is a colour | Because it is the name of a boat | *To make the speech sound old-fashioned and grand
exam: «The ship's cook checked the victuals before the crew set sail.» What does victuals mean? | Letters from home | *Food and drink for the voyage | Sharp tools for the deck | Warm clothes for winter
